# Spell lessons (playtest 2, N3): once a spell is learned, the next term month shows where and how it's really taught,
# and by whom. One card per spell, once a run. Written for the current twelve spells; reworked if the minigames change them.

@event lesson_flame
title: Putting Fire Out
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(flame) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: thorne
art: flame
tags: magic study
scene: A stone practice room with scorched walls, a stern grey-bearded professor beside a roaring brazier, a bucket of water at his feet.
text: Flame is yours now. Professor Thorne gathers the year's new Flame casters in the stone practice room and lights a brazier. "Any fool can start a fire," he says. "You are here to learn to stop one." There is a bucket of water at his feet, and he forbids you to touch it.
> left: Pull the fire back into your hand
  meet thorne
  arcana +small
  mana -small
  say: You call the flame back like a sleeve pulled over a wrist. It stings, and the brazier flares and sinks. "Again," says Thorne. He says it eleven times.
> right: Ask what the bucket is for
  meet thorne
  lore +small
  rel thorne +small
  say: "For the student who doesn't ask," says Thorne, and empties it over the brazier of the boy beside you. Then he explains, for an hour, how every great fire he has known began.

@event lesson_tide
# retired by spells v2 (was "spell_level(tide) >= 1 and term"): tide is cut
title: Waist-Deep at Dawn
trigger: auto
when: false
class: routine
tone: light
actor: marisol
art: wave
tags: magic study retired
scene: A misty lake at dawn, a professor standing waist-deep in the water with arms raised, students hesitating on the pebbled shore.
text: Professor Marisol teaches Tide where it lives: waist-deep in the lake at dawn. "Cold water," she says happily, "is good for everyone." She recites a poem about waves and stops before the last line, frowning. The lake waits for you.
> left: Wade in and call a wave
  meet marisol
  arcana +small
  health -tiny
  say: The water is so cold it feels like an opinion. Your wave rises knee-high and slaps Marisol full in the chest, and she whoops. "The last line!" she shouts. "It was about this!"
> right: Call it from the shore
  meet marisol
  lore +small
  rel marisol -tiny
  say: You work the spell from the dry pebbles and raise a neat little swell. Marisol looks at your dry boots with real sorrow, as if at a poem with no ending.

@event lesson_foresight
title: The Bottom of the Cup
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(foresight) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: warm
art: eye
tags: magic study
scene: An observatory room with tea cups on every desk, a lecturer in three scarves peering into a student's cup with a magnifying glass.
text: The astromancy lecturer, who wears three scarves in every season, sits the class at the observatory with cups of tea and no instructions. Foresight, it turns out, begins at the bottom of the cup: what you think you see, and what you want to.
> left: Read your own cup
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: The leaves say a hard week is coming. It does, from Tuesday. The lecturer says that's the trouble with reading your own: you start making it true.
> right: Read your neighbour's
  charm +small
  say: You tell your neighbour a letter is on its way. A letter arrives at supper. Now half the dormitory wants their leaves read, and you have to learn to say "unclear" kindly.

@event lesson_starlight
title: A Jar on the Roof
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(starlight) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: warm
slot: night
art: star
tags: magic study
scene: A flat observatory roof at night, a student holding up a glass jar toward a sky crowded with stars, breath steaming.
text: Starlight is practised after midnight on the observatory roof, where your Starcalling tutor hands you a glass jar and tells you to hold it up "until something falls in". The stars are very far away. The roof is very cold.
> left: Hold the jar until it fills
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: At three in the morning a thread of light settles in the jar like milk in tea. You carry it down the stairs in both hands. You can't feel your fingers until breakfast.
> right: Lie back and watch the sky
  mood +small
  say: You lie on the roof and name the constellations with whoever is beside you. Your jar stays empty. The lecturer, passing, says that's the first lesson too, and the one most people skip.

@event lesson_mend
title: Teacups First
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(mend) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: amaranth
art: cup
tags: magic study
scene: An infirmary storeroom, a stern healer dropping a teacup to the stone floor while a student kneels over the pieces with a wand.
text: Sister Amaranth teaches Mend with teacups. She drops one on the infirmary floor without looking. "Mend it." When it's whole she drops another. "Bones are harder," she says, rolling bandages. "Cups first."
> left: Mend cup after cup
  meet amaranth
  alchemy +small
  mana -small
  say: By the twentieth cup you can hear the crack close before you see it. Amaranth says "Keep doing that," and drops a teapot.
> right: Ask her about the bones
  meet amaranth
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: She tells you, still rolling bandages, about the worst break she ever set. It was a student's; that student is a professor now. You go a little pale, and listen to every word.

@event lesson_purify
title: Make It Stronger
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(purify) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: amaranth
art: cauldron
tags: magic study
scene: An alchemy lab bench with a cauldron of thick green liquid, a healer standing over a student who holds a wand above it.
text: Sister Amaranth has heard that Pell taught you to Distill. She sets a cauldron of something green in front of you that she will not name. "Pell teaches it fast," she says. "I teach it so you're still standing afterwards. Make it stronger." Then she goes back to her own work.
> left: Distill it drop by drop
  meet amaranth
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: Drop by drop, the green comes down to one dark spoonful, and each drop costs you a little. You sit down halfway, as she said you would. Amaranth tastes the result, nods and puts it on the high shelf.
> right: Ask what it was
  meet amaranth
  rel amaranth +small
  say: "Last term's soup," she says. You decide not to believe her, then decide you do. She shows you where to stop before it takes too much from you, and you distill half a cup, badly.

@event lesson_glamour
title: Pick a Card
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(glamour) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: vey
art: cards
tags: magic study
scene: A velvet-curtained classroom, a professor in a violet coat fanning out a hand of playing cards toward a row of students.
text: Professor Vey's glamour lesson is a card game. Each student glamours a plain card to look like the queen of stars, and Vey picks out the true queen from the pile. Nobody, she says, has ever made her pick wrong. "Fool me, and you may skip the essay."
> left: Glamour your card
  meet vey
  challenge deceive master- unlikely +1 if spell_level(glamour) >= 2:
    win:
      renown +medium
      rel vey +medium
      charm +small
      if enrolled(illusion):
        study illusion +large
      else:
        charm +small
      say: Vey picks your card, turns it over and finds a plain two. She laughs out loud, a real laugh, and the class goes silent. "Well," she says. "Well, well. No essay for you." By supper the whole tower knows, and Vey has started calling you "my card sharp".
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      guile +small
      say: Vey passes over your card without a glance. "The queen of stars," she says kindly, "does not blush." But you saw how she chose: by the hands, not the cards.
> right: Watch how she catches them
  meet vey
  guile +small
  say: You watch her eyes instead of the cards. She never looks at the faces, only at the hands holding them. You write that down, and she sees you write it, and smiles.

@event lesson_mirror
# retired by spells v2 (was "spell_level(mirror) >= 1 and term"): mirror is cut
title: Gently
trigger: auto
when: false
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: vey
art: mirror
tags: magic study retired
scene: A dim classroom with the curtains drawn, a hand mirror on every desk, a professor standing well to one side of the glass.
text: Professor Vey teaches Mirror in an empty classroom with the curtains drawn, which is not like her. "A mirror charm shows people themselves," she says. "Do it gently. Most people have not asked to see." She stands to one side of every glass in the room.
> left: Practise on yourself first
  meet vey
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: The charm shows you yourself a moment too honestly. You practise until you can look at it without flinching. Mostly.
> right: Ask why she avoids the glass
  meet vey
  rel vey +small
  say: "Vanity," says Vey, with a magnificent smile that answers nothing at all. But she lets you stay after the others leave, and shows you how to soften the charm's edges.

@event lesson_haste
title: Race the Hippogriff
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(haste) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: brask
art: feather
tags: magic study
scene: A windy flight field marked with flags, a grey-haired professor holding a stopwatch, a student sprinting beside a hippogriff.
text: Professor Brask teaches Haste on the flight field, with a stopwatch and a hippogriff that is faster than the spell. "Beat her," she says to you. To the hippogriff she explains, at length and politely, that she is not to bite you if you win.
> left: Race the hippogriff
  meet brask
  challenge feat adept-:
    win:
      vigor +small
      rel brask +tiny
      say: You cross the flag a beak's length ahead. The hippogriff looks at Brask; Brask looks at the stopwatch, then at you. "Hm," she says. From her, it's a medal.
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      say: The hippogriff wins by a length and takes your hat as her prize. Brask tells a story about a student who lost to a goose. It ends with a bite. "Moral: faster."
> right: Time the others first
  meet brask
  lore +small
  say: You hold the stopwatch and watch the fast ones. They aren't running harder; they start the spell a step before they need it. Brask sees you notice, and hands you the next race.

@event lesson_beast_speech
# retired by spells v2 (was "spell_level(beast_speech) >= 1 and term"): beast_speech is cut
title: The Mule Has Views
trigger: auto
when: false
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: brask
art: paw
tags: magic study retired
scene: A warm stall with straw on the floor, a student sitting cross-legged opposite a grey old mule who stares back without blinking.
text: Beast-speech, says Professor Brask, is mostly listening. She sits you in a stall beside an old mule who has, she says, "views". You are to hear them. The mule looks at you as if you're the one being taught.
> left: Sit with him all afternoon
  meet brask
  vigor +small
  mood -tiny
  say: In the fourth hour the mule's thoughts come through: mostly apples, and one long grievance about a farrier. Brask says "Good," to the mule, not to you.
> right: Try the spell straight away
  meet brask
  arcana +small
  mana -small
  say: You cast before you've listened and get one word back, very clearly: "Rude." Brask laughs once, loudly. It's the laugh of the term, and everyone in the stables heard it.

@event lesson_ward
title: Chalk
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(ward) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: shield
tags: magic study
scene: A dim room at the top of a tower, low lamps, a lecturer mid-throw with a piece of chalk while students hold up shimmering wards.
text: At the top of the lecture tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose, the umbral lecturer drills first-years in their Ward by throwing chalk. Hard, and without warning. "A ward that only works when you're ready," says the lecturer, "is a decoration."
> left: Hold the ward all hour
  guile +small
  mana -small
  say: The first chalk hits your forehead. The fifth bounces off the ward with a sound like a bell. By the end of the hour your ward is up before you know you've raised it.
> right: Watch the lecturer's wrist
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: You let the ward drop and watch how the throws begin. Three pieces of chalk find your forehead while you do. But you see it now: the shoulder moves first.

@event lesson_shadowstep
title: Where the Voice Was
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(shadowstep) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: tense
time: night
art: moon
tags: magic study
scene: A pitch-dark tower room with one dim lamp guttering out, the outline of a student stepping between deep shadows.
text: Shadowstep is taught in the dark. The umbral lecturer puts out the last dim lamp and calls your name from somewhere else in the room. You have to arrive where the voice was before it moves. Somewhere, a chair is waiting for your shins.
> left: Step into the dark
  guile +small
  health -tiny
  say: You step, and the dark folds, and you're across the room with a bruise from the chair. The voice is right beside you. "Better," it says, and moves away again.
> right: Wait for your eyes to adjust
  lore +small
  say: You stand still until the dark has edges. The lecturer, somewhere, says that's cheating, and that cheating is half of umbral magic. You step only once, but you step exactly.

# ---------------- Spells v2: practice for the new spells (one each, after you learn it) ----------------

@event lesson_animate
title: The Chair That Follows
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(animate) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: spell_animate
tags: magic study
scene: A workshop corridor, a wooden chair trotting after a student like a dog, a teapot waddling behind it.
text: The Artifice homework is to animate something and live with it for a day. You pick a chair, which follows you to every lecture. The teapot is the other option. It has opinions about being carried.
> left: Live with the chair
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: The chair follows you everywhere and offers itself whenever you stop, including on the stairs, at speed. By evening you have bruised shins and a very loyal chair.
> right: Animate the teapot instead
  arcana +small
  mana -small
  say: The teapot waddles after you all day, pouring when you sit. It takes more out of you than the chair would, but your friends have never been so well looked after.

@event lesson_seal
title: Only for the Addressee
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(seal) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: eerie
art: spell_seal
tags: magic study
scene: A windowless room, a student pressing their thumb to a folded letter while a headmistress watches with her hands folded.
text: Today's Hollow Studies exercise: seal a letter so that only the person it's addressed to can open it. The headmistress will try to open it first. "If I can," she says mildly, "you haven't."
> left: Seal it against her
  guile +small
  mana -small
  say: She tries the letter for a long minute with one finger, then hands it back unopened. "Better than I was at your age," she says, which doesn't sound like a compliment. You feel hollowed out till supper.
> right: Ask how she'd break it
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: She shows you, kindly and very quickly, how she'd break yours. You leave the room knowing a great deal more about seals and rather less sure of your own.

@event lesson_wildfire
title: Longer Each Night
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(wildfire) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: tense
actor: lyra
art: spell_wildfire
tags: magic duel
scene: An empty duel hall at night, two students each holding a growing fire, one watching the other's with narrowed eyes.
text: Lyra wants you in the duel hall after hours. "You let it go too early," she says, and lights her own. "Hold it. Longer each night. It will bite you; let it."
> left: Hold it until it bites
  arcana +small
  health -tiny
  say: You hold it until the heat is in your teeth and then a breath longer. When you let it go it runs to the rafters. Lyra looks at your blistered palm and says nothing, which is approval.
> right: Ask her how she stands it
  rel lyra +small
  mood -tiny
  say: She doesn't answer at first. Then she shows you her palms, scarred and old, and tells you about the first time, and her mother. You don't practise much. It's still the right night.

@event lesson_heartwood
title: A Tree by the Window
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(heartwood) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: tobias
art: spell_heartwood
tags: magic nature
scene: A dormitory courtyard at dusk, a young tree that wasn't there that morning, two students sitting under it looking pleased with themselves.
text: Tobias wants you to grow a heartwood every evening for a week, "so your hands remember". Today he suggests the courtyard under your dormitory window, where nothing has grown in years.
> left: Grow it properly, roots and all
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: It takes everything you've got and a blister. By dusk there's a young tree under your window, and your back aches like a harvest. Tobias says it's the best one he's seen, and apologises for saying so.
> right: Sit under his instead
  mood +small
  rel tobias +tiny
  say: You let him grow it and sit under it with him, talking about nothing. Your hands don't remember much. The rest of you does.

@event lesson_omen
title: The Spoon Will Fall
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(omen) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: eerie
actor: seraphine
art: spell_omen
tags: magic study
scene: A refectory table at breakfast, a pale student pointing at a spoon balanced on the edge of a bowl, a second student staring at it.
text: At breakfast Seraphine says, without looking up, "Practise on small things. Spoons. Doors. The weather. Never people, not yet." Then she looks at you, quite clearly, for once.
> left: Foretell small things all day
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: The spoon falls. The door sticks. It rains at four. By evening it unnerves you how well it works, and you catch yourself foretelling the soup.
> right: Ask what "not yet" means
  rel seraphine +small
  say: "Later," she says, then, "I mean that exactly." She doesn't explain. She does pass you the salt before you've asked for it.

@event lesson_sleight
title: Pockets and Decks
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(sleight) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: corvin
art: spell_sleight
tags: magic
scene: A dim corner of a common room, a grinning student fanning a deck of cards while another student palms a coin under the table.
text: Corvin is teaching you to keep your hand in. "Decks are practice," he says, shuffling. "Pockets are performance." Bram is asleep in an armchair nearby, his whistle on a ribbon round his neck.
> left: Practise on the deck
  guile +small
  mood -tiny
  say: Two hours of coins and cards until your fingers cramp. It's dull, and by the end the coin vanishes so cleanly even Corvin blinks. "Saint," he says, "with very quick hands."
> right: Lift Bram's whistle and return it
  guile +medium
  alignment -tiny
  say: The whistle comes off its ribbon without a sound and goes back on it the same way. Bram snores through all of it. Corvin is delighted and slightly worried about you.

@event lesson_contraption
title: The Breakfast Engine
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(contraption) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: mir
art: spell_contraption
tags: magic
scene: A breakfast table where a clattering machine made of a toast rack, a spoon and a teapot is buttering toast at alarming speed.
text: Mir slides a breakfast tray across to you. "Practice! Make it do breakfast. Toast, butter, tea. It's the hardest machine there is, probably." Half the refectory has already turned to watch.
> left: Build the breakfast engine
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: Your engine butters four slices, pours one cup and singes your eyebrow before it falls apart in a shower of crumbs. The refectory applauds. Mir writes something down, beaming.
> right: Build it with Mir, slowly
  rel mir +small
  mood +tiny
  say: You build it together, Mir explaining every joint. It makes one perfect slice of toast and then retires. Mir says it's the best machine of the year, probably.

@event lesson_hoarfrost
title: Putting Out Fires
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(hoarfrost) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: kael
art: spell_hoarfrost
tags: magic duel
scene: A duel hall where one student's flame is frosting over and going out, its caster glaring, a quiet student standing nearby.
text: "It's for putting fires out," Kael says again, and nods across the duel hall to where Lyra is practising, very loudly, with a very large flame. He waits to see whether you'll take the hint.
> left: Put out Lyra's flame
  arcana +small
  rel lyra -tiny
  say: Your frost meets her flame and they cancel with a hiss and a cloud of steam. Lyra's face, when it clears, is magnificent. Kael makes a sound you think is a laugh.
> right: Practise on the braziers instead
  arcana +tiny
  rel kael +tiny
  say: You frost the braziers one by one. It's useful, and dull, and Kael stays with you for all of it without saying anything. You suspect that was the point.

@event lesson_candour
title: Questions People Want to Answer
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(candour) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: nessa
art: spell_candour
tags: magic social
scene: A busy refectory, a bossy editor pointing out students at random tables to another student holding a notebook.
text: Nessa hands you a notebook in the refectory. "Three people, three true answers, by the end of lunch," she says. "Ask the question they want to answer. Use the spell only when you have to."
> left: Use the spell on all three
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: Three truths by the pudding course, and one of them is about you, and not kind. Nessa reads your notes and says, "Now you know why I don't use it much."
> right: Ask good questions instead
  rel nessa +small
  charm +tiny
  say: You only manage two truths without the spell, but they're good ones, and nobody minded telling you. Nessa says that's the better trick, and that she won't print either.

@event lesson_brace
title: Planted
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(brace) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: spell_brace
tags: magic duel
scene: A duel hall's practice corner, an eyepatched dummy firing weak hexes at a student who stands planted and unmoving.
text: The eyepatched dummy is empty now, but it still throws a hex or two out of habit. The Circle uses it to practise bracing. Today the seniors are taking bets on how long you'll stand.
> left: Stand until it gives up
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: You stand there for an hour while the dummy throws everything it has left. The seniors pay out to whoever bet longest. Your shins are a mess and you are unmovable.
> right: Coach the first-years instead
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: You stand behind a row of first-years and fix their feet, one by one, while they get hexed. They're grateful. You're bored. The dummy throws one at you on the way out, out of spite.

@event lesson_quickquill
title: Lecture Notes
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(quickquill) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: spell_quickquill
tags: magic study
scene: A lecture hall, a quill writing busily by itself in a notebook while its owner sits back with folded arms, the lecturer squinting at them.
text: Your quill wants to take your lecture notes for you. It writes everything down, word for word, faster than the lecturer can talk. It also writes what it thinks of the lecturer, in the margins.
> left: Let it take the notes
  study +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: You have perfect notes, every word, and a margin full of the quill's views on the lecturer's waistcoat. You spend the evening inking them out before anyone sees.
> right: Make it take only what you say
  guile +small
  mana -tiny
  say: It sulks, and then takes down exactly what you tell it, no more. It's work keeping it honest. You come out with half the notes and the full trust of your quill.

@event lesson_bramble
title: A Hedge on Demand
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(bramble) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: spell_bramble
tags: magic nature
scene: Old Maud's cottage garden, a groundskeeper pointing at a gap in her fence while a student coaxes a thorny hedge up out of the ground to fill it.
text: Old Maud has heard about your hedge. "There's a gap in my fence the rabbits use, dearie," she says. "Grow me one there. Thorns on the outside, mind." The rabbits are watching from the cabbages.
> left: Grow it thorns and all
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: The hedge comes up thick and thorny, and takes a strip off your knuckles on the way. Maud inspects it and says it'll do. The rabbits look betrayed.
> right: Grow it gently, for the cabbages
  rel maud +small
  mood +tiny
  say: You grow it soft and plant a row of cabbages beyond it for the rabbits. Maud laughs until she has to sit down. "You'll understand that in two weeks," she says.

@event lesson_index
title: The Right Page
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(index) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: spell_index
tags: magic study
scene: A library table piled with heavy books, every one falling open at a different marked page as a student touches it, a cat watching from the top of the pile.
text: Every book you touch falls open at the page you need, now. The trouble is the library knows it, and books are leaving their shelves to come and find you. Footnote is sitting on the biggest.
> left: Read every page they offer
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: You read every page that offers itself until the lamps go out. You learn a great deal, most of it useful, and none of it what you came in for.
> right: Find one thing for your exams
  study +tiny
  say: You ask for one thing, firmly. Twelve books bring it. You take your page and leave the others, who sulk on the table until a clerk shelves them.

@event lesson_rapport
title: A Room That Was Turning
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(rapport) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: spell_rapport
tags: magic social
scene: A refectory at supper where two tables are on the edge of an argument, a student stepping between them with a raised mug and a grin.
text: Two tables in the refectory are on the edge of a food fight, and every head is turning. It's the Newt all over again, with worse gravy.
> left: Step in and turn it
  renown +small
  mood -tiny
  say: One joke about the gravy, at exactly the right moment, and both tables laugh instead of throwing it. The prefects look relieved and slightly suspicious. Your heart doesn't slow down until bed.
> right: Let it happen
  charm +tiny
  mood +small
  say: You let it go, and the food fight is magnificent. You watch from under the table, laughing. Nobody remembers it was nearly stopped.

@event lesson_tailwind
title: Never Stop
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(tailwind) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
art: spell_tailwind
tags: magic
scene: A student on a broom skimming low over a lake at tremendous speed, a wake of spray behind them and a fisherman waving from a boat.
text: The fisherman's rule for the wind is "never stop". You take the long way round the lake on your free afternoon to practise it, and the wind gets behind you as soon as you're off the jetty.
> left: Never stop
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: You go round the lake three times without stopping, faster each lap, until your hands are numb on the handle. The fisherman waves every lap now.
> right: Land and talk to him
  mood +small
  charm +tiny
  say: You land by his boat and tell him about the storm run. He listens, nods, and says, "Never stop," again, and hands you a fish to take home.

# ---------------- The Whisperwood's spells (world expansion 1) ----------------
# Four hidden spells found in the wood, each recognised by someone at the academy, and six forbidden ones nobody
# will teach: their "lesson" is the first quiet moment you could use one, and whether you do.

@event lesson_lodestar
title: The Lighthouse Light
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(lodestar) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: grimsby
art: spell_lodestar
tags: magic
scene: An old bookseller in a dark shop watching a soft guiding light gather around a student's shoulders, his spectacles reflecting it.
text: The light gathers round your shoulders in Grimsby's shop without your meaning it to, and the old man puts down his pen. "Lodestar," he says softly. "A lighthouse keeper's spell. She used it to walk the lamp-stair in storms." He doesn't say who she was. You think you know.
> left: Ask him to watch you cast it
  meet grimsby
  arcana +small
  mana -tiny
  say: You let the light build, breath by breath, until it stands round you like a lantern. Grimsby nods along to your breathing, as if to a tune he knows. "Slower," he says. "She was always slower."
> right: Ask about the lighthouse
  meet grimsby
  lore +small
  rel grimsby +tiny
  say: He tells you about the lamp, the stair, the keeper who wanted to see a long way, and the books she bought from him for forty years. The light round your shoulders stays lit the whole time, listening.

@event lesson_moonwell
title: Sitting Still
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(moonwell) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: maud
art: spell_moonwell
tags: magic
scene: An old woman and a student sitting on a garden bench under the moon, eyes closed, a faint silver light pooling in the student's cupped hands.
text: Old Maud finds you on her garden bench at dusk, trying to make Moonwell go faster. She sits down beside you. "That's your trouble," she says. "It's a sitting-still spell, dearie. You can't hurry a basin."
> left: Sit still with her
  meet maud
  arcana +small
  health +tiny
  say: You sit until the moon is up and your feet are cold. Somewhere in the second hour, the light starts pooling, and you stop trying, and it fills. Maud is asleep. You don't wake her.
> right: Ask how she knows the spell
  meet maud
  lore +small
  rel maud +tiny
  say: "Learned it in the wood," says Maud, "same as you, sixty years back. Everybody who goes deep enough learns it eventually. The ones who come back, anyway." She pats your knee as if that were reassuring.

@event lesson_reflect
title: Hall of Mirrors
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(reflect) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: light
actor: vey
art: spell_reflect
tags: magic
scene: A theatrical professor in a corridor of mirrors throwing a small spell at a student, who sends it bouncing back down the corridor.
text: Professor Vey hears you've learned Reflect and appears in the mirror corridor with a bag of very small, very harmless hexes. "Show me," Vey says, and throws one at your head.
> left: Send it back
  meet vey
  arcana +small
  mana -tiny
  say: You catch the hex and return it. Vey ducks, delighted, and throws three. You return two. The third gives you rabbit ears for an hour, which Vey says is the lesson.
> right: Send it somewhere else
  meet vey
  guile +small
  rel vey +tiny
  say: You catch the hex and bounce it off a mirror into a suit of armour, which sneezes. Vey laughs so hard the mirrors ring. "Oh, you're going to be trouble."

@event lesson_rime
title: Cold Enough
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(rime) >= 1 and term and met(kael)
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: kael
art: spell_rime
tags: magic
scene: A pale Northern student and another student standing in a frost-whitened courtyard, frost spreading from their collars, breath steaming.
text: Kael sees the frost at your collar and goes still. "Rime," he says. "Where did you... no. Don't tell me." He looks at {season == "winter": the snow|the courtyard, and frost runs out from his boots across the flagstones}. "In the North we learn it as children. You don't feel the cold less. You just stop letting it decide things."
> left: Let him show you
  meet kael
  arcana +small
  health -tiny
  say: He makes you stand in {season == "winter": the snow|the cold he's laid on the flagstones} until it hurts, and then until it doesn't, and then a while longer. When you go in, he's almost smiling. "Now it's yours," he says.
> right: Ask what else they learn as children
  meet kael
  rel kael +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: "Not to want things," he says, and then looks as if he wishes he hadn't. He leaves before you can ask anything else. The frost at your collar feels colder.

@event lesson_siphon
title: The Fern on the Sill
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(siphon) >= 1 and term
slot: night
class: routine
tone: grim
art: spell_siphon
tags: magic forbidden
scene: A student at a dormitory window at night with a hand hovering over a potted fern, the fern's leaves beginning to curl.
text: You're tired, and there are lectures at eight, and there's a fern on your windowsill, green and full of life it isn't using. Siphon is right there in your hands. Nobody would ever know but the fern.
> left: Just a little
  alignment -small
  health +small
  say: The fern greys from the tips inward. You feel wonderful. You throw the fern out in the morning, before anyone sees it.
> right: Go to bed tired
  alignment +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: You go to bed tired, and wake tired, and the fern is green. You water it, which feels like an apology to something.

@event lesson_compel
title: "Again"
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(compel) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: grim
art: spell_compel
tags: magic forbidden
scene: A student in a castle corridor watching another student drop a stack of books, a word forming on their lips.
text: A first-year drops a stack of books in the corridor, and laughs at themselves, and you could say the word. "Again." They'd drop them again, and again, and never know why. It would be funny. That's the worst part: it would be funny.
> left: Say it, once
  alignment -small
  guile +small
  say: They drop the books again, and stare at their hands. You walk on quickly. It wasn't funny.
> right: Help pick them up
  alignment +tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: You pick up half the books and the first-year thanks you twice. The word stays in your mouth, unused, like a stone you could throw.

@event lesson_torment
title: The Shape of It
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(torment) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: grim
actor: thorne
art: spell_torment
tags: magic forbidden
scene: A stern grey-bearded professor in a duelling hall looking hard at a student whose wand hand has frozen mid-cast.
text: Sparring in the duelling hall, you feel Torment rise in your hand like a reflex. You stop it. Professor Thorne, across the hall, saw something: he's looking at you the way he looks at a fire in the wrong place.
> left: Tell him
  meet thorne
  alignment +small
  rel thorne +tiny
  mood -small
  say: You tell him. He listens without a word, then says, "Then you know what it costs. Most people learn that afterwards." He gives you extra drills for a month, which you suspect is kindness.
> right: Say it was nothing
  meet thorne
  guile +tiny
  rel thorne -tiny
  say: "Nothing, sir." He doesn't believe you. He doesn't say so. He watches you spar for the rest of the term.

@event lesson_extraction
title: Someone Else's Spark
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(extraction) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: grim
art: spell_extraction
tags: magic forbidden
scene: A study group around a library table at night, one student's quill glowing with magic, another student staring at it hungrily.
text: In a study group, the student beside you is casting a little light to read by, and you can feel their magic like warmth from a stove. Extraction would take it. They'd feel tired, that's all. You've got so much to do.
> left: Take a little
  alignment -small
  mana +small
  say: Their light gutters. They yawn, rub their eyes, and go to bed early. You study until dawn on someone else's magic, and remember none of it.
> right: Lend them yours instead
  alignment +tiny
  mana -tiny
  say: You light the table yourself so they can rest their hand. They grin at you. The hunger goes quiet, for now.

@event lesson_eclipse
title: The Burn
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(eclipse) >= 1 and term
class: routine
tone: grim
art: spell_eclipse
tags: magic forbidden
scene: A student in an alchemy lab clutching a burnt hand, a faint dark glow gathering around the injury.
text: You burn your hand on a cauldron in the lab, badly. And Eclipse wakes up: the pain could be power, it says, all of it, held and ready. You'd still feel the burn. You'd just have something to show for it.
> left: Hold the pain
  alignment -small
  arcana +small
  health -tiny
  say: You let the burn become power and carry it all day like a coal in your pocket. It's very strong. It still hurts. Both of those stay true.
> right: Go to the infirmary
  alignment +tiny
  health +tiny
  say: Sister Amaranth dresses it and tells you off for daydreaming. The power you didn't take aches a little, like a tooth.

@event lesson_heartprice
title: Paying in Yourself
trigger: auto
when: spell_level(heartprice) >= 1 and term
slot: night
class: routine
tone: grim
art: spell_heartprice
tags: magic forbidden
scene: An exhausted student at a desk late at night, a faint red glow in their chest feeding a spell, candles burnt down.
text: It's late, {mana < 15: your Mana is spent|your Mana is low and tomorrow has a practical}, and the essay isn't done. Heartprice says you don't need Mana. You have yourself. You could pay in that, for as long as you can stand it.
> left: Pay in yourself
  alignment -tiny
  study +tiny
  health -small
  say: You finish the essay on your own strength. In the morning your hands shake and the essay is very good. You don't feel proud of it.
> right: Leave it for tomorrow
  study -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: You go to bed with the essay unfinished. It's worse than it would have been. You're better than you would have been.
